<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:system="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime">

    <!--
        How wide every column starts, and how narrow any of them may get. Nothing else.

        SPLIT OUT OF Values.xaml ON 2026-08-17, owner's decision. Values.xaml answers "how big is
        a gap, a letter, a mark", Colours.xaml answers "what colour", and this answers "how wide
        is that column" - three questions somebody arrives with separately, which is what makes
        each name answerable without opening the file.

        WHY IT WAS SPLIT AT ALL. Values.xaml stood exactly on the markup ceiling of 436 and the
        ratchet may only go down, so every new column and every new mark was refused by the build
        until somebody found a seam. Backlog 189 carries the decision and 173 the measurement:
        eighteen of that file's entries were column widths.

        NOTHING IN ANY STYLE POINTS AT THESE KEYS, and that was checked rather than assumed before
        the split. Every one of them is read from CODE, by the WidthKey on each entry of
        ViewModels/Columns.cs, because S6d2 builds the columns rather than declaring them. So the
        merge order that the other value files depend on does not decide anything here - a
        StaticResource is resolved while a file is read, and there is no StaticResource on any of
        these. It is still merged with the other value files, because that is where a value file
        belongs and because a reader should not have to know this paragraph to place it.
    -->

    <!--
        COLUMN WIDTHS, AND THE THREE FIXED ONES ARE FIXED BECAUSE Auto WAS MEASURED AND IT LIES.

        Measured 2026-08-05 with tools/gui-probe/columns.ps1, which drives the real window:
        Width="Auto" on a DataGrid that virtualises its rows sizes to the rows REALISED SO FAR
        and does not measure again as more scroll into view. The PID column sat at 36 pixels
        through all 811 rows while the widest process id on screen went from 14052 to 103292 -
        which needs 62 - so five and six digit numbers were cut in half by their own cell. That
        is backlog item 53, and it is the whole of it: nothing ever overflowed the viewport, the
        sum of the columns equalled it to the pixel in all five states measured.

        The same run answered item 1 as a side effect. Account is declared 2* in MainWindow.xaml
        and the grid reported 1.57514450867052* - the grid REWRITES star factors to pay for Auto
        columns growing. Four attempts at tuning these proportions failed because the numbers
        written in the view were not the numbers in force.

        The fixed three are sized to their widest possible content plus the mark, not to today's
        machine. The starred three carry text that will never fit at any width - a display name
        wanted 476 pixels - so they get an ellipsis and a floor instead.

        These are here rather than in the view because a width is an appearance value, ADR-23,
        and because the columns are about to become a list somebody can add to and hide from.
    -->
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnName">2*</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnDisplayName">3*</DataGridLength>
    <!--
        The widest starred factor there is, because this column carries PROSE rather than a label
        and the longest measured is 1251 characters - no width fits it, so the only question is
        how much of a sentence arrives before the ellipsis. Off at the start, so it costs the
        default view nothing.
    -->
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnDescription">4*</DataGridLength>

    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnStatus">150</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnStart">215</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnAccount">2*</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnProcessId">72</DataGridLength>

    <!--
        THE ELEVEN COLUMNS `A8` PROMISES AND NOBODY COULD TURN ON UNTIL 2026-08-11, and every
        width here is now a STARTING width rather than the last word. That distinction is the
        sentence added to `ADR-23` the same day: the theme decides where a column begins, and a
        person dragging its edge decides where it ends up, because S6d2 made the edge draggable.
        Without that sentence this file and the layout would both claim to own the same number.

        FIXED OR STARRED IS DECIDED THE SAME WAY THE FIRST SIX WERE. A column whose widest
        possible content is known gets a number, because Width="Auto" measures the rows realised
        so far and never measures again - that is backlog 53 and it cost a slice. A column
        carrying text that will never fit at any width gets a star, an ellipsis and the floor
        above, because something has to give and the person has to see that it did.

        These starting numbers are ESTIMATES FROM THE HEADING AND THE VOCABULARY, not
        measurements, and saying so is the difference between a number and a guess wearing a
        number's clothes. The fixed ones are sized to the longest word each column can hold -
        "File system driver", "Service SID type", "Against its start type" - at the semibold
        heading size, which is larger than the data. tools/gui-probe/columns.ps1 is what measures
        them on a real window, and it is a step of tools/check.ps1.
    -->
    <!--
        THE TWO COLUMNS THAT COST NO NEW READING, added 2026-08-17 on the owner's report that the
        window shows too few columns. Both fields arrive in the buffers the listing already fills
        and both were already ON SCREEN - folded into the start column as "Automatic (delayed)" and
        "file missing". A column each makes them sortable and filterable by eye, which a qualifier
        inside another cell can never be.

        Fixed, and sized to the heading rather than to the data, because the data is Yes or No and
        the heading is the widest thing either column will ever hold. Estimates from the vocabulary,
        like the eleven below - tools/gui-probe/columns.ps1 is what measures them on a real window.
    -->
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnDelayedAuto">120</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnBinaryOnDisk">130</DataGridLength>

    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnEntryType">150</DataGridLength>

    <!-- Sized to the heading rather than to the data, like the two above it: the widest value is
         "Not per-user" and the heading "Per-user role" is longer than any of the three. -->
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnPerUserRole">130</DataGridLength>

    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnAgainstStartType">175</DataGridLength>

    <!-- The other direction of the same disagreement - backlog 170. -->
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnRunsWhileDisabled">175</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnBinaryPath">3*</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnBinaryFile">3*</DataGridLength>

    <!-- The second phase of ADR-13, backlog 21. The hash is a fixed sixty-four characters and is
         given room for them: a truncated hash is not a shorter answer, it is a different one. -->
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnSignature">130</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnPublisher">2*</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnFileVersion">130</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnBinaryHash">4*</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnMemory">110</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnLoadOrderGroup">2*</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnErrorControl">120</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnSidType">140</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnDependsOn">2*</DataGridLength>

    <!-- The other direction, 2026-09-06. Same width as its pair, because they hold the same shape of value. -->
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnRequiredBy">2*</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnTriggers">2*</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnPrivileges">2*</DataGridLength>
    <DataGridLength x:Key="ColumnDescriptor">3*</DataGridLength>

    <!--
        What a starred column may shrink to before it stops being worth reading. Below this the
        ellipsis is most of the cell.

        WHAT HAPPENS ONCE IT REACHES THIS FLOOR CHANGED ON 2026-08-12, and the sentence that stood
        here said the opposite: that the whole row stayed on screen and long text gave way, because
        the owner had chosen no sideways scrolling on 2026-08-05. He reversed that, so a row whose
        starred columns are all on the floor now gets WIDER THAN THE WINDOW and gains a scrollbar.
        Measured with all seventeen columns on: 1922 pixels of row behind a 1039 viewport.

        A FIXED WIDTH IS ITS OWN FLOOR, and that is not written here because it is not a number -
        ListColumns says it, because it says the width and the floor are the same value. Before it
        did, the seven fixed columns were the ones that gave way, all the way to twenty pixels.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="ColumnFloor">90</system:Double>

    <!--
        CEILINGS, AND ONLY WHERE THE CONTENT HAS ONE. A share keeps growing with the window, which
        is right for a column that can always use more room and wrong for one that cannot - past a
        point the extra is empty ground in the middle of a row, and it pushes the description away
        from the name it belongs to.

        FOUND 2026-09-08 BY PHOTOGRAPHING THE WINDOW MAXIMISED, which had never been done - every
        picture this project had of its own window until that day was at the size it opens at or
        SMALLER, while the owner keeps it maximised. The numbers came from columns.ps1, which has
        measured the maximised state all along: nobody had looked at the pixels it stood for.

        Measured at a viewport of 2516 against 1041 at the default size:

          Display name   717 wide, longest text on this machine 409  ->  308 spare
          Account        478 wide, longest text on this machine 199  ->  279 spare
          Description    956 wide, longest text 7488                 ->  starved, and cut

        So 587 of 2516 - 23% of the window - stood empty in two columns that had stopped needing
        it, while the one column that can always use more was being ellipsised.

        THESE TWO NUMBERS ARE HEADROOM OVER A MEASUREMENT, NOT THE MEASUREMENT. 480 is 17% over the
        longest display name here and 280 is 41% over the longest account. A machine with longer
        text gets an ellipsis, which is what it already gets at the default size, where these
        columns are 225 and 150 - well under both ceilings. NOTHING HERE CHANGES THE DEFAULT SIZE.

        No ceiling on Description, on a path or on a hash: their content has no natural width, so
        every pixel given to them is used.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="ColumnDisplayNameCeiling">480</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="ColumnAccountCeiling">280</system:Double>
</ResourceDictionary>
